Hutchinson's paradox of the plankton concerns the apparent contradiction between two facts: (i) the principle of competitive exclusion asserts that, at equilibrium, the number of coexisting species cannot exceed the number of limiting resources, so when many phytoplankton species compete for only one or a few limiting nutrients, only one or a few species should survive; (ii) in real aquatic ecosystems hundreds of phytoplankton species are observed to coexist while competing for only one or a few limiting nutrients.

Construct and analyze a nonequilibrium reaction–advection–diffusion model of the following form. Let f(t,x) ≥ 0 denote the density of a single limiting nutrient and let ρ_n(t,x) ≥ 0, n = 1, 2, …, N, denote the densities of N distinct phytoplankton species, where x lies in a bounded fluid domain Ω ⊂ ℝ^d (d = 2 or 3) with suitable boundary conditions. Suppose:

(a) the water carries a prescribed (possibly turbulent) velocity field u(t,x) advecting both the nutrient and the species;
(b) each species n drifts relative to the water with a species-specific velocity v_n(t,x), modeling buoyancy or motility;
(c) the nutrient is regenerated by a source term S(t,x,f) and consumed by uptake of the form Σ_n U_n(f) ρ_n, where U_n is a species-dependent uptake function (e.g. of Monod type);
(d) species n grows at a rate G_n(f) ρ_n − m_n ρ_n with species-dependent growth function G_n and mortality m_n;
(e) molecular or eddy diffusion with coefficients D_f, D_n ≥ 0 is included.

The coupled system is, schematically,
  ∂_t f + ∇·(u f) = D_f Δf + S(t,x,f) − Σ_n U_n(f) ρ_n,
  ∂_t ρ_n + ∇·((u + v_n) ρ_n) = D_n Δρ_n + (G_n(f) − m_n) ρ_n,  n = 1,…,N.

Question: Can such a nonequilibrium model exhibit, for natural choices of u, v_n, S, U_n, G_n, sustained long-time coexistence of an arbitrarily large number N of species (in the sense that, for example, lim inf_{t→∞} ∫_Ω ρ_n(t,x) dx > 0 for every n), thereby providing a mechanistic resolution of the paradox of the plankton? More precisely, identify conditions on the velocity fields, nutrient regeneration, uptake and growth functions under which competitive exclusion fails and persistent coexistence of many species occurs.

